WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
Good to have you here for first principles daily, episode 11.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.580
Today, we reason from raw materials, not analogy,

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.220
transmission lines that could cost a few times

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.060
their copper and steel now run many times that

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.700
because the real expense sits in permits, cues,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.800
and custom builds.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.360
A new high voltage transmission line in the United States

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.120
today can cost several million dollars per mile.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.260
The copper, aluminum, and steel inside it

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.640
represent only a modest fraction of that total.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.320
Generation costs have fallen sharply in recent years,

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.280
yet the wires and transformers that move power

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.560
remain hostage to slow approvals, fragmented ownership,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.220
and one-off engineering.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.200
First principles reasoning starts by asking

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.680
what the raw material floor actually is.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.940
It then asks why the finished price sits so far above that floor.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.620
Utilities and developers still plan and build transmission

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.980
the way they have for decades.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.440
Each project begins with a bespoke root study.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.940
That study is followed by years of environmental reviews,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.020
land negotiations, and state-by-state regulatory

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.480
filings come next.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.700
Right-of-way acquisition alone can stretch across

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.640
dozens of private parcels and multiple jurisdictions.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.980
Each jurisdiction brings its own hearing calendar

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.080
and appeal process.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.240
Once approvals arrive, engineering proceeds line-by-line.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.740
Towers, conductors, and foundations

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.280
are specified for local soil, wind, and ice loads

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.620
rather than drawn from a standard catalog.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.160
Transformers and substations follow the same pattern.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.600
Most units are engineered to order.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.220
Led times run in years because manufacturing capacity

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.540
itself is allocated through long-term utility contracts.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.480
Interconnection cues add another layer.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.500
A generator seeking to connect must study impacts

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.920
on every neighboring line and substation.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.620
The generator must then fund upgrades

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.600
whose scope and cost are negotiated case-by-case.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.300
These steps compound over time.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.000
Each delay raises financing costs.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.760
Each custom specification raises fabrication cost.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.240
Each multi-party coordination meeting adds overhead

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.540
that never appears in the bill of materials.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.700
The result feels normal inside the industry.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.760
Every participant has optimized

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.540
within the existing sequence rather than questioning

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:19.680
the sequence itself.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.540
Consider a representative 500 kilovolt alternating current line.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.360
A rough magic wand estimate begins

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.020
with the major material streams.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.880
Those streams include aluminum conductor, steel core,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.800
or lattice, concrete foundations, and insulators.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.280
Commodity prices for aluminum and steel fluctuate.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.520
Published market data plays the contained metal value

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.700
for a typical double-circuit mile in the low six figures

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.740
when measured at recent averages.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.960
Adding the concrete, hardware, and insulators

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.380
brings the raw material floor to perhaps

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.880
a few hundred thousand dollars per mile.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.780
This remains an estimate that ignores forming energy

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.580
and transport.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.620
Published project costs for recent US lines

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.180
range from roughly one to several million dollars per mile.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.540
Once land, engineering, and construction are included.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.280
Dividing those reported totals by the material floor

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.840
produces an idiot index on the order of five to 15.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.880
The exact value depends on terrain and regulatory burden.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.060
The gap does not sit in the conductor itself.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.080
It lives in the multi-year permitting sequence.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.360
It lives in the carrying cost of capital during that weight.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.440
It lives in the custom foundation designs for each tower site.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.200
It lives in the incremental studies required for interconnection.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.760
Each of those stages multiplies expense

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.360
without adding proportional physical material.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.660
Advanced conductors that can carry more power on existing towers

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.460
illustrate the same arithmetic at smaller scale.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.400
The material upgrade itself is modest.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.240
Yet the avoided cost of new right of way

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.900
and new towers can be large.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.860
Those items dominate the idiot index.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.700
A redesign would attack the highest multiplier steps first.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.460
Standardized tower families and foundation modules

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.180
pre-qualified across common soil types

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.760
could shrink engineering hours.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.580
They could allow factories to stock rather than build to order.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.080
Advanced conductors and grid enhancing technologies

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.720
such as power flow controllers would increase capacity

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.660
on corridors already permitted.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.920
This lowers the effective idiot index

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.440
by raising throughput without new land.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.420
High voltage direct current links could move large blocks

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.120
of power over long distances with fewer lines

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.760
and narrower rights of way.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.660
Converter stations themselves would need

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.020
to move down their own cost curve through modular designs.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.120
On the interconnection side, pre-computed hosting capacity

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.040
maps and automated study processes could replace

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.680
sequential project by project analysis.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.980
None of these moves removes the hard constraints

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:03.740
of citing wildlife corridors or community opposition.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.380
Those remain genuine limits set by physics and politics.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.020
Yet each change attacks a different term in the cost stack.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.620
If permitting timelines compress and fabrication shifts

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.040
toward repeatable modules, the idiot index can fall

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.340
even while the raw material floor stays roughly constant.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.820
The prize is measured not in eliminating every dollar

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.240
above the floor, but in moving the ratio

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.080
from double digits towards something

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.760
closer to the material limit.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.260
A line whose price greatly exceeds its contained metal

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.620
is announcing a process problem, not a shortage of copper.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.360
The sequence begins with route studies that take years,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.040
then moves through fragmented land deals

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.960
and repeated environmental reviews.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.500
After that comes custom engineering for every tower

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.100
and foundation, followed by made to order transformers

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.640
that sit in long queues.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.820
Interconnection studies then layer on still more case

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.500
by case analysis and negotiated upgrades.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.920
Each of these steps multiplies the final price

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.960
without adding much extra material.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.480
Questioning the sequence of approvals and custom builds

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.760
before optimizing the conductor itself

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.140
is the first step toward closing the gap.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.680
The same pattern appears wherever intermediate steps

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.640
have accreted without re-examination.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.640
Tomorrow we will examine another domain

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.340
where the raw material floor and the finished price

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.020
have drifted far apart.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.980
Who will be the first to publish a full cost stack

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.000
for a standardized transmission corridor

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.220
and show the arithmetic in public?

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.840
That's first principles daily for today.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.620
If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.760
share it with someone who depreciated

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.560
and subscribe wherever you listen.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.440
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, one example

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.160
or one opportunity every day.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:50.300
See you tomorrow.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.540
And if you'd rather watch than listen,

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:55.540
find us on YouTube at at Nerra Network.

